 "E25"}, {"evidence_id": "E26", "source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170572404968", "text_context_id": "E26"}, {"evidence_id": "E27", "source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170571649704", "text_context_id": "E27"}, {"evidence_id": "E28", "source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170571611384", "text_context_id": "E28"}, {"evidence_id": "E29", "source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170571669700", "text_context_id": "E29"}, {"evidence_id": "E30", "source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170572559812", "text_context_id": "E30"}], "assessment": {"requirements": [{"requirement_index": 0, "status": "SUPPORTED", "citations": [{"source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170571688085", "quote": "As we have seen, we may evaluate easily the limits of polynomials and limits of some (but not all) rational functions by direct substitution. However, as we saw in the introductory section on limits, it is certainly possible for munder(lim, x→a)f(x) to exist when f(a) is undefined. The following observation allows us to evaluate many limits of this type:"}], "rationale": "We may evaluate easily the limits of polynomials and some rational functions by direct substitution."}, {"requirement_index": 1, "status": "SUPPORTED", "citations": [{"source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170571649704", "quote": "The limit has the form munder(lim, x→a)((f(x))/(g(x))), where munder(lim, x→a)f(x)=0 and munder(lim, x→a)g(x)=0. (In this case, we say that f(x)/g(x) has the indeterminate form 0/0.) The following Problem-Solving Strategy provides a general outline for evaluating limits of this type."}], "rationale": "A limit of the form (f(x))/g(x) where limₓ→a f(x)=0 and limₓ→a g(x)=0 is identified as having the indeterminate form 0/0."}, {"requirement_index": 2, "status": "SUPPORTED", "citations": [{"source": "background", "id": "background", "quote": "Rational arithmetic, elementary algebra including rearrangement, and ordinary reading comprehension. Geometry formulas, function concepts and exponential rules require source or target evidence."}], "rationale": "Rational arithmetic and elementary algebra including rearrangement are provided."}, {"requirement_index": 3, "status": "SUPPORTED", "citations": [{"source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170571611384", "quote": "Calculating a Limit When f(x)/g(x) has the Indeterminate Form 0/0 First, we need to make sure that our function has the appropriate form and cannot be evaluated immediately using the limit laws. We then need to find a function that is equal to h(x)=f(x)/g(x) for all x≠a over some interval containing a. To do this, we may need to try one or more of the following steps: If f(x) and g(x) are polynomials, we should factor each function and cancel out any common factors. If the numerator or denominator contains a difference involving a square root, we should try multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the expression involving the square root. If f(x)/g(x) is a complex fraction, we begin by simplifying it. Last, we apply the limit laws."}], "rationale": "When evaluating a limit of the form limₓ→a f(x)/g(x) with an indeterminate 0/0, if f and g are polynomials you may factor them and cancel any common factors before applying the limit laws."}, {"requirement_index": 4, "status": "SUPPORTED", "citations": [{"source": "passage", "id": "m53492:fs-id1170571611384", "quote": "Calculating a Limit When f(x)/g(x) has the Indeterminate Form 0/0 First, we need to make sure that our function has the appropriate form and cannot be evaluated immediately using the limit laws. We then need to find a function that is equal to h(x)=f(x)/g(x) for all x≠a over some interval containing a. To do this, we may need to try one or more of the following steps: If f(x) and g(x) are polynomials, we should factor each function and cancel out any common factors. If the numerator or denominator contains a difference involving a square root, we should try multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the expression involving the square root. If f(x)/g(x) is a complex fraction, we begin by simplifying it. Last, we apply the limit laws."}], "rationale": "If f(x) and g(x) are polynomials, we should factor each function and cancel out any common factors."}], "additional_requirements": [], "input_readable": true}, "task_facts": {"status": "SUFFICIENT", "missing_facts": [], "rationale": "The target gives the limit point x→1/2 and the rational function (2x^2+3x−2)/(2x−1). Background supplies elementary algebra for evaluating at the point and factoring. Source rules identify the 0/0 indeterminate form and allow factoring and canceling common factors to compute the limit, so all needed facts are present."}, "review_context": [{"id": "T0", "text": "In the following exercises, use direct substitution to show that each limit leads to the indeterminate form 0/0. Then, evaluate the limit.

munder(lim, x→1/2)((2(x)^(2)+3x−2)/(2x−1))"}, {"id": "B0", "text": "Rational arithmetic, elementary algebra including rearrangement, and ordinary reading comprehension. Geometry formulas, function concepts and exponential rules require source or target evidence."}, {"id": "G0", "text": "In the following exercises, use direct substitution to show that each limit leads to the indeterminate form 0/0. Then, evaluate the limit."}, {"id": "G1", "text": "munder(lim, x→1/2)((2(x)^(2)+3x−2)/(2x−1))"}, {"id": "E0", "text": "Now that we have reviewed the basic characteristics of functions, we can see what happens to these properties when we combine functions in different ways, using basic mathematical operations to create new functions. For example, if the cost for a company to manufacture x items is described by the function C(x) and the revenue created by the sale of x items is described by the function R(x), then the profit on the manufacture and sale of x items is defined as P(x)=R(x)−C(x). Using the difference between two functions, we created a new function."}, {"id": "E1", "text": "Alternatively, we can create a new function by composing two functions. For example, given the functions f(x)=(x)^(2) and g(x)=3x+1, the composite function f∘g is defined such that"}, {"id": "E2", "text": "(f∘g)(x)=f(g(x))=((g(x)))^(2)=((3x+1))^(2)."}, {"id": "E3", "text": "The composite function g∘f is defined such that"}, {"id": "E4", "text": "(g∘f)(x)=g(f(x))=3f(x)+1=3(x)^(2)+1."}, {"id": "E5", "text": "By allowing for quotients and fractional powers in polynomial functions, we create a larger class of functions. An algebraic function is one that involves addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, rational powers, and roots. Two types of algebraic functions are rational functions and root functions."}, {"id": "E6", "text": "Just as rational numbers are quotients of integers, rational functions are quotients of polynomials. In particular, a rational function is any function of the form f(x)=p(x)/q(x), where p(x) and q(x) are polynomials. For example,"}, {"id": "E7", "text": "f(x)=((3x−1)/(5x+2))andg(x)=((4)/((x)^(2)+1))"}, {"id": "E8", "text": "are rational functions. A root function is a power function of the form f(x)=(x)^(1/n), where n is a positive integer greater than one. For example, f(x)=(x)^(1/2)=sqrt(x) is the square-root function and g(x)=(x)^(1/3)=root(3, x) is the cube-root function. By allowing for compositions of root functions and rational functions, we can create other algebraic functions. For example, f(x)=sqrt(4−(x)^(2)) is an algebraic function."}, {"id": "E9", "text": "Let’s look at how to derive the first equation. The others follow similarly. Suppose y=(sinh)^(−1)x. Then, x=sinhy and, by the definition of the hyperbolic sine function, x=(((e)^(y)−(e)^(−y))/(2)). Therefore,"}, {"id": "E10", "text": "(e)^(y)−2x−(e)^(−y)=0."}, {"id": "E11", "text": "Multiplying this equation by (e)^(y), we obtain"}, {"id": "E12", "text": "(e)^(2y)−2x(e)^(y)−1=0."}, {"id": "E13", "text": "For the following problems, state the domain and range of the given functions:"}, {"id": "E14", "text": "f=(x)^(2)+2x−3,g=ln(x−5),h=((1)/(x+4))"}, {"id": "E15", "text": "Looking at a table of functional values or looking at the graph of a function provides us with useful insight into the value of the limit of a function at a given point. However, these techniques rely too much on guesswork. We eventually need to develop alternative methods of evaluating limits. These new methods are more algebraic in nature and we explore them in the next section; however, at this point we introduce two special limits that are foundational to the techniques to come."}, {"id": "E16", "text": "Two Important Limits Let a be a real number and c be a constant. munder(lim, x→a)x=a munder(lim, x→a)c=c"}, {"id": "E17", "text": "We can make the following observations about these two limits."}, {"id": "E18", "text": "We now take a look at the limit laws, the individual properties of limits. The proofs that these laws hold are omitted here."}, {"id": "E19", "text": "Limit Laws Let f(x) and g(x) be defined for all x≠a over some open interval containing a. Assume that L and M are real numbers such that munder(lim, x→a)f(x)=L and munder(lim, x→a)g(x)=M. Let c be a constant. Then, each of the following statements holds: Sum law for limits: munder(lim, x→a)(f(x)+g(x))=munder(lim, x→a)f(x)+munder(lim, x→a)g(x)=L+M Difference law for limits: munder(lim, x→a)(f(x)−g(x))=munder(lim, x→a)f(x)−munder(lim, x→a)g(x)=L−M Constant multiple law for limits: munder(lim, x→a)cf(x)=c·munder(lim, x→a)f(x)=cL Product law for limits: munder(lim, x→a)(f(x)·g(x))=munder(lim, x→a)f(x)·munder(lim, x→a)g(x)=L·M Quotient law for limits: munder(lim, x→a)((f(x))/(g(x)))=((munder(lim, x→a)f(x))/(munder(lim, x→a)g(x)))=((L)/(M)) for M≠0 Power law for limits: munder(lim, x→a)((f(x)))^(n)=((munder(lim, x→a)f(x)))^(n)=(L)^(n) for every positive integer n. Root law for limits: munder(lim, x→a)root(n, f(x))=root(n, munder(lim, x→a)f(x))=root(n, L) for all L if n is odd and for L≥0 if n is even and f(x)≥0."}, {"id": "E20", "text": "We now practice applying these limit laws to evaluate a limit."}, {"id": "E21", "text": "By now you have probably noticed that, in each of the previous examples, it has been the case that munder(lim, x→a)f(x)=f(a). This is not always true, but it does hold for all polynomials for any choice of a and for all rational functions at all values of a for which the rational function is defined."}, {"id": "E22", "text": "Limits of Polynomial and Rational Functions Let p(x) and q(x) be polynomial functions. Let a be a real number. Then, munder(lim, x→a)p(x)=p(a) munder(lim, x→a)((p(x))/(q(x)))=((p(a))/(q(a)))whenq(a)≠0."}, {"id": "E23", "text": "To see that this theorem holds, consider the polynomial p(x)=(c)_(n)(x)^(n)+(c)_(n−1)(x)^(n−1)+⋯+(c)_(1)x+(c)_(0). By applying the sum, constant multiple, and power laws, we end up with"}, {"id": "E24", "text": "As we have seen, we may evaluate easily the limits of polynomials and limits of some (but not all) rational functions by direct substitution. However, as we saw in the introductory section on limits, it is certainly possible for munder(lim, x→a)f(x) to exist when f(a) is undefined. The following observation allows us to evaluate many limits of this type:"}, {"id": "E25", "text": "If for all x≠a,f(x)=g(x) over some open interval containing a, then munder(lim, x→a)f(x)=munder(lim, x→a)g(x)."}, {"id": "E26", "text": "To understand this idea better, consider the limit munder(lim, x→1)(((x)^(2)−1)/(x−1))."}, {"id": "E27", "text": "The limit has the form munder(lim, x→a)((f(x))/(g(x))), where munder(lim, x→a)f(x)=0 and munder(lim, x→a)g(x)=0. (In this case, we say that f(x)/g(x) has the indeterminate form 0/0.) The following Problem-Solving Strategy provides a general outline for evaluating limits of this type."}, {"id": "E28", "text": "Calculating a Limit When f(x)/g(x) has the Indeterminate Form 0/0 First, we need to make sure that our function has the appropriate form and cannot be evaluated immediately using the limit laws. We then need to find a function that is equal to h(x)=f(x)/g(x) for all x≠a over some interval containing a. To do this, we may need to try one or more of the following steps: If f(x) and g(x) are polynomials, we should factor each function and cancel out any common factors. If the numerator or denominator contains a difference involving a square root, we should try multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the expression involving the square root. If f(x)/g(x) is a complex fraction, we begin by simplifying it. Last, we apply the limit laws."}, {"id": "E29", "text": "The next examples demonstrate the use of this Problem-Solving Strategy. [reference:#fs-id1170571669713] illustrates the factor-and-cancel technique; [reference:#fs-id1170572307613] shows multiplying by a conjugate. In [reference:#fs-id1170571612021] , we look at simplifying a complex fraction."}, {"id": "E30", "text": "We now turn our attention to evaluating a limit of the form munder(lim, x→a)((f(x))/(g(x))), where munder(lim, x→a)f(x)=K, where K≠0 and munder(lim, x→a)g(x)=0. That is, f(x)/g(x) has the form K/0,K≠0 at a."}]}

